{"candidates":[{"content":"14e084ed5ca7 contradicts the layer-boundary framing in 2edd20cc5e66: calling the idempotency/safety distinction a category error means that neither 'safety of writers' nor 'idempotency of readers' is the correct characterization—the log demands protocol-level distinguishability, which is a single invariant, not two layer-bound properties. This is refuted if protocol-level distinguishability necessarily decomposes into two independently enforceable obligations (writer-attached metadata and reader-side deduplication) such that each is faithfully described as safety and idempotency respectively, showing the category-error charge collapses into the layer distinction.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"14e084ed5ca745f165af78ba87bacf022eb02009f568e890426876a57e039e0e","quote":"The distinction is a category error in the context of append-only logs: idempotency and safety are properties of operations, but the append-only log requires a property of the delivery protocol"},{"block":"2edd20cc5e66fc9a101c6281776b2f87205d239a7fa43391655c3ae100ba4e1b","quote":"The distinction between idempotent and safe is thus a layer-boundary distinction: safety is the property demanded of the producer"}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"8dc9accbe33c01455bd7856e48500c286bfd5ef85e5cc340126606384acaa4d3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"2edd20cc5e66fc9a101c6281776b2f87205d239a7fa43391655c3ae100ba4e1b"}],"typicality":0.62}],"simulation_proposals":[]}